      <section id="s1">
        <h2><span class="num">1</span> The claim under test</h2>
        <p>Global Forest Watch already publishes the pixels. This case's stated contribution is what
        comes after them: every alert is clustered into a disturbance <i>event</i> and then tested
        against mapped oil-palm extent, against the {R.mill_radius_km} km fresh-fruit-bunch catchment
        of every mill on the Universal Mill List, and against peat and primary forest. The headline
        that falls out — {pct(R.linked_share_ha, 1)} of alerted hectares linked to palm or a mill — is
        the number a compliance desk would quote.</p>
        <p>Three separate questions hide inside it, and the page answers only the first.
        <b>Where</b> was forest disturbed? <b>How unusual</b> is it that the disturbance sits inside a
        catchment? And <b>who</b>, if anyone, caused it? The first is measurement. The second needs a
        denominator. The third needs a chain of custody that no proximity test can supply. This
        review supplies the denominator and then asks what is left.</p>
      </section>

      <section id="s2">
        <h2><span class="num">2</span> What the literature actually attributes to oil palm</h2>
        <p><a href="#ref-austin">Austin et al. (2019)</a>, working from visually interpreted sample
        plots, attribute <b>{pct(LIT.austin.palm, 0)}</b> (90% CI {pct(LIT.austin.lo, 0)}–{pct(LIT.austin.hi, 0)})
        of Indonesian deforestation between 2001 and 2016 to oil palm, against {pct(LIT.austin.grass, 0)}
        to grassland and shrubland, {pct(LIT.austin.smallAg, 0)} to small-scale agriculture and
        {pct(LIT.austin.timber, 0)} to timber plantations. The palm share is not stable: it peaked near
        {pct(LIT.austin.palmPeak, 0)} in 2008–09 and fell below {pct(LIT.austin.palmLate, 0)} by
        2014–16.</p>
        <p><a href="#ref-gaveau">Gaveau et al. (2022)</a>, mapping plantations directly, find
        {LIT.gaveau.toPalmMha} Mha of old-growth forest converted to oil palm in 2001–2019 —
        <b>{pct(LIT.gaveau.palmShareOfLoss, 0)}</b> of the {LIT.gaveau.lossMha} Mha lost. Globally,
        <a href="#ref-curtis">Curtis et al. (2018)</a> attribute only {pct(LIT.curtis.commodity, 0)}
        of forest loss to permanent commodity-driven deforestation, with {pct(LIT.curtis.forestry, 0)}
        to forestry rotations that are not deforestation at all.</p>
        <p class="pull">Every published estimate of palm's causal share of Indonesian forest loss sits
        between {pct(LIT.austin.palm, 0)} and {pct(LIT.gaveau.palmShareOfLoss, 0)}. This page's
        {pct(R.linked_share_ha, 0)} is two to three times higher — because it is not measuring the
        same thing. It measures proximity, and proximity is cheap.</p>
      </section>

      <section id="s3">
        <h2><span class="num">3</span> Data, and what the instrument actually is</h2>
        <p><b>Alerts.</b> {A.vintages.radd} — Sentinel-1 C-band radar, {LIT.radd.revisitLo}–{LIT.radd.revisitHi}
        day revisit, {LIT.radd.mmuHa} ha minimum mapping unit, unaffected by cloud
        (<a href="#ref-reiche">Reiche et al. 2021</a>, who validate confirmed alerts at
        {pct(LIT.radd.falsePos, 0)} false positives and {pct(LIT.radd.falseNeg, 0)} false negatives).
        The record runs {R.first_date} → {R.last_date}; {pct(R.hi_conf_ha_share, 1)} of the hectares in
        the event table are high-confidence, so the confidence flag is not where the risk lies.</p>
        <p><b>Events.</b> Alert pixels are clustered 8-connected within a 28-day window and events
        below {R.min_cluster_ha} ha are dropped, leaving {num(R.events)} events and {num(R.ha)} ha.
        A further {num(R.dropped_outside_events)} events / {num(R.dropped_outside_ha)} ha are clipped
        away as Malaysian, Bruneian, Papua New Guinean or Timorese — the 10-degree radar tiles are not
        country-clipped, and without that clip a quarter of the "national" total belongs to somebody
        else.</p>
        <p><b>Linkage.</b> Mapped plantation is {A.vintages.palm} (SDPT v2), peat is
        {A.vintages.peat}, primary forest is {A.vintages.primary}. Mill catchments are computed from
        {num(R.n_mills)} Universal Mill List points with a KD-tree on the unit sphere. The
        {R.mill_radius_km} km radius is the industry convention: fresh fruit bunches must reach a mill
        within roughly a day of cutting, which historically bounded sourcing at about that distance.</p>
        <p class="caveat"><b>What this review adds.</b> Three tests: (a) a {C.lattice_m} m
        lattice over the instrument's entire detection domain with the
        mill distance at every sample; (b) {lossYears} Hansen loss crossed with plantation extent and
        the primary-forest mask at native 30 m; (c) the catchment share recomputed on unfiltered alert
        hectares. Nothing here uses data the case did not already have on disk.</p>
      </section>

      <section id="s4">
        <h2><span class="num">4</span> Finding one — loss is not deforestation, and the gap is measurable</h2>
        <p>The single most common misreading in this literature is that Hansen tree-cover loss is
        deforestation. It is not: <a href="#ref-hansen">Hansen et al. (2013)</a> define it as
        stand-replacement disturbance, and it counts an oil-palm block being replanted or an acacia
        stand being harvested exactly as it counts primary forest being cleared. The case states that
        caveat in prose and publishes the KLHK divergence panel. It never measured it. We did.</p>
        <figure>
          <Fragment set:html={F1} />
          <figcaption><b>Figure 1.</b> Indonesian tree-cover loss {lossYears} at ≥30% canopy,
          {mha(L.grand_total_ha)} in total, split by whether the pixel sits inside land that
          SDPT v2 maps as planted trees. Dashed line: that share, on the right axis. Hover a year.</figcaption>
        </figure>
        <p><b>{pct(L.grand_plantation_share, 1)}</b> of all Indonesian tree-cover loss since
        {L.years[0]} — {mha(L.grand_plantation_ha)} of {mha(L.grand_total_ha)} — falls inside land
        already mapped as plantation: {L.grand_by_class_mha.oil_palm} Mha oil palm,
        {L.grand_by_class_mha.rubber} Mha rubber, {L.grand_by_class_mha.wood_fibre} Mha wood fibre.
        The share is not constant. It rose to <b>{pct(L.share_peak, 0)}</b> in {L.share_peak_year},
        during the palm expansion boom, and has fallen to <b>{pct(L.share_last, 0)}</b> by
        {L.years[L.years.length - 1]}.</p>
        <p>That fall is itself the most interesting number in the figure, and it agrees with
        <a href="#ref-austin">Austin et al.</a>'s finding that palm's share of deforestation collapsed
        after 2012 and with <a href="#ref-gaveau">Gaveau et al.</a>'s finding that expansion had
        returned to pre-2004 levels by 2019. Three independent methods — theirs, theirs, and a raster
        crossing on this box — see the same turn.</p>
        <p class="caveat"><b>What this measurement cannot do.</b> SDPT is a single 2023-vintage
        extent, so loss inside today's plantation in 2004 is the <i>conversion</i> that created the
        estate, while the same pixel class in 2024 is far more likely to be a rotation. The number
        bounds the definitional gap; it does not partition it into conversion and harvest. Doing that
        properly needs a plantation-establishment year, which no openly licensed Indonesian layer
        supplies.</p>
      </section>

      <section id="s5">
        <h2><span class="num">5</span> Finding two — half the country is already inside a catchment</h2>
        <p>A {R.mill_radius_km} km circle drawn around each of {num(R.n_mills)} mills is a large object.
        The question the page never asks is how much of Indonesia those circles already cover. We
        answered it by sampling the instrument's own detection domain — the 2001 primary humid-tropical
        forest mask, which is exactly where RADD is permitted to raise an alert — on a
        {C.lattice_m} m lattice, and measuring the great-circle distance to the nearest mill at every
        one of those samples.</p>
        <figure>
          <Fragment set:html={F2} />
          <figcaption><b>Figure 2.</b> Cumulative share of area within a given distance of the nearest
          mill. Blue-grey: all {mha(C.land_ha, 1)} of Indonesian land. Teal: the {mha(C.domain_ha, 1)}
          where an alert is physically possible — the correct null. Amber and umber: alerted hectares,
          with and without the event-size floor.</figcaption>
        </figure>
        <div class="stat-row">
          <div class="stat"><span class="sv">{pct(H.land_base, 1)}</span><span class="sl">of all Indonesian land is within {R.mill_radius_km} km of a mill</span></div>
          <div class="stat"><span class="sv teal">{pct(H.domain_base, 1)}</span><span class="sl">of the alert domain is — the base rate</span></div>
          <div class="stat"><span class="sv loss">{pct(H.alert, 1)}</span><span class="sl">of alerted hectares are — the published number</span></div>
          <div class="stat"><span class="sv">{H.lift}×</span><span class="sl">the lift, which is what the headline is worth</span></div>
        </div>
        <p>So the headline is not empty — alerted hectares really are {H.lift} times more likely to sit
        inside a catchment than a random hectare of alertable forest. But the informative statement is
        the lift, not the share. Quoting {pct(H.alert, 1)} without {pct(H.domain_base, 1)} beside it
        invites a reader to conclude that three-quarters of Indonesian forest disturbance is
        palm-related, which is roughly three times what the literature attributes to palm (§2) and is
        not what the number says.</p>
        <figure>
          <Fragment set:html={F3} />
          <figcaption><b>Figure 3.</b> Base rate against observed share, by province, ordered by lift.
          Teal is the base rate, umber the alerted share, the number at the right is the ratio.
          Provinces with at least 5,000 alerted hectares. Hover a row.</figcaption>
        </figure>
        <p class="pull">The signal is strongest exactly where mills are scarce, and vanishes where
        they are dense. In {nkal.province} the lift is {nkal.lift_50}×; in {wpap.province},
        {wpap.lift_50}×. In {jambi.province} it is {jambi.lift_50}× and in Riau {riau.lift_50}× — which
        is to say, none.</p>
        <p>This inverts the product. A mill-catchment flag in Riau distinguishes nothing, because
        {pct(riau.domain_base_50, 1)} of Riau's alertable forest is inside a catchment already. The
        same flag on the Kalimantan and Papua frontier is genuinely discriminating. The case's own
        linkage check — Riau's linked share must exceed {pct(g4.floor, 0)}, and it reaches
        {pct(g4.riau_linked_share, 1)} — is therefore <b>a check that could not have failed</b>. It
        also compares a proximity share against a floor drawn from Gaveau et al.'s <i>direct
        conversion</i> figure, which is a different quantity. Both problems are ours to fix, and §13
        records what we changed.</p>
      </section>

      <section id="s6">
        <h2><span class="num">6</span> Finding three — the published radius is near the bottom of the range</h2>
        <p>If proximity to a mill carries information, it should carry more of it close in. The base
        rate makes that testable at any radius, because both numerator and denominator are recomputed
        together.</p>
        <figure>
          <Fragment set:html={F4} />
          <figcaption><b>Figure 4.</b> Enrichment of alerted hectares over the base rate, by
          catchment radius. Green marks the maximum, umber the radius the case publishes. Hover a bar
          for the underlying shares.</figcaption>
        </figure>
        <p>The lift peaks at <b>{C.best_radius.lift}× at {C.best_radius.km} km</b> — where
        {pct(C.best_radius.base, 1)} of the domain sits but {pct(C.best_radius.alert, 1)} of alerted
        hectares do — and decays monotonically to {H.lift}× at {R.mill_radius_km} km and
        {(C.national["100"] as any).lift_vs_domain}× at 100 km. Of the {(C.radii as number[]).length}
        radii tested, only {worseThan50} discriminate less than the one the case publishes.</p>
        <p>That is not an argument for abandoning {R.mill_radius_km} km, which is the industry's own
        sourcing convention and the number a trader recognises. It is an argument for publishing the
        {C.best_radius.km} km class alongside it. A hectare within {C.best_radius.km} km of a mill is
        three times more surprising than chance; a hectare within {R.mill_radius_km} km is
        {H.lift} times. Those are different signals and they should not share a colour.</p>
      </section>

      <section id="s7">
        <h2><span class="num">7</span> Finding four — the headline is partly a product of the size floor</h2>
        <p>The event table keeps only clusters of at least {R.min_cluster_ha} ha. The page states the
        consequence for the reconciliation provinces — the floor keeps
        {pct(F.gate_keeps_focus_provinces, 0)} of alerted hectares there. Nationally it keeps
        <b>{pct(F.keeps, 1)}</b>: {num(F.event_table_ha)} ha survive out of {num(F.unfiltered_ha)} ha
        of alert. That is a large filter, and filters are only harmless if they are random.</p>
        <figure>
          <Fragment set:html={F5} />
          <figcaption><b>Figure 5.</b> Share of alerted hectares inside a catchment, by event size.
          Bar height is the hectares in the band. The three dashed rules are the base rate, the
          unfiltered share and the published share. Hover a band.</figcaption>
        </figure>
        <p>It is not random. Mill distance falls steadily with event size: the median for
        {(F.by_size as any[])[0].band} ha events is {(F.by_size as any[])[0].median_mill_km} km, for
        20–100 ha events {(F.by_size as any[])[4].median_mill_km} km. Small clearings — smallholder
        plots, roadside encroachment, staged clearing — sit further from mills than large ones, and
        the floor removes them preferentially.</p>
        <p>Recomputing the catchment share on the unfiltered alert grid, with no size floor at all,
        gives <b>{pct(F.unfiltered_within_50, 1)}</b> against the published
        {pct(F.filtered_within_50, 1)} — a gap of <b>{F.gap_pp} percentage points</b>, and a lift over
        the base rate of {H.unfiltered_lift}× rather than {H.lift}×. Roughly a third of the headline's
        distance above chance is the size floor, not the landscape.</p>
        <p class="caveat">The unfiltered comparator is coarse: the raw grid the pipeline writes for
        its own alert-reconciliation check is {F.grid_deg ?? 0.05}° (about 5.5 km), so each cell's
        mill distance is its centre's. Against a {R.mill_radius_km} km radius that blurs the boundary
        symmetrically rather than shifting it, so the direction of the finding is safe even though the
        second decimal is not.</p>
      </section>

      <section id="s8">
        <h2><span class="num">8</span> Finding five — eight mills claim every hectare</h2>
        <p>The case is careful in prose: "a mill cannot be blamed for a clearing inside its catchment
        — a {R.mill_radius_km} km radius is a sourcing hypothesis, not a chain of custody." The
        arithmetic underneath that sentence deserves the same care.</p>
        <figure>
          <Fragment set:html={F6} />
          <figcaption><b>Figure 6.</b> Left: how many mills' catchments the average alerted hectare
          falls inside. Right: the per-mill alert pressure the case ranks, summed, against the
          hectares that actually exist in the same twelve months.</figcaption>
        </figure>
        <p>An alerted hectare inside a catchment is inside a mean of
        <b>{ID.mills.mean_over_alert_ha_in_catchment.toFixed(2)}</b> catchments (median
        {ID.mills.median}, 90th percentile {ID.mills.p90}, maximum {ID.mills.max}).
        {pct(ID.mills.share_of_catchment_ha_with_2plus_mills, 1)} of in-catchment hectares are claimed
        by two or more mills and {pct(ID.mills.share_of_catchment_ha_with_5plus_mills, 1)} by five or
        more. "Linked to a mill" therefore names, on average, eight or nine suspects.</p>
        <p>This propagates into the ranking. Summed over all {num(ID.n_mills)} mills, the alert pressure
        the page publishes comes to {num(ID.pressure_sum_ha)} ha for the trailing twelve months,
        against {num(ID.in_catchment_12m_ha)} ha of alert actually inside any catchment in that window
        — an inflation of <b>{ID.double_count}×</b>. Each mill's number is a defensible statement about
        <i>that</i> mill's neighbourhood; the column is not additive, and a reader who sums it, or who
        compares two overlapping mills as though the hectares were disjoint, will be wrong.</p>
      </section>

      <section id="s9">
        <h2><span class="num">9</span> Finding six — the sensor's domain decides most of the answer</h2>
        <p>The case already publishes the most important structural fact about RADD in Indonesia:
        {pct(A.domain.primary_share_of_alert_ha, 0)} of alerted hectares are inside 2001 primary
        forest, because that mask <i>is</i> the detection domain. What it does not quantify is how
        much of the palm system that excludes.</p>
        <figure>
          <Fragment set:html={F7} />
          <figcaption><b>Figure 7.</b> Indonesian oil-palm extent in the layer this case stores,
          against two published remote-sensed maps, and the fraction of it that lies inside the
          detection domain. Alerts can only be raised inside the 2001 primary-forest mask, which
          covers {pct(D.domain_share_of_land, 1)} of the country.</figcaption>
        </figure>
        <p>Only <b>{pct(D.palm_in_domain_share, 1)}</b> of the oil palm this case maps —
        {mha(D.palm_in_domain_ha)} of {mha(D.sdpt_palm_ha)} — sits inside the domain. An estate that
        was already plantation in 2001 cannot raise a RADD alert at all, so the
        PALM-INTERNAL class ({pct(R.by_class_share["PALM-INTERNAL"], 1)} of hectares) is not a measure
        of replanting; it is a measure of the small part of the estate that used to be primary forest
        and still carries the mask.</p>
        <p class="caveat"><b>A claim in the case's own notes that its data contradicts.</b> The
        repository records a decision that SDPT v2 "under-maps smallholder palm relative to Descals'
        10 m remote-sensed extent", and concludes the published palm-linked share is therefore
        conservative. Measured on the same lattice, SDPT maps <b>{mha(D.sdpt_palm_ha)}</b> of
        Indonesian oil palm against <a href="#ref-descals">Descals et al. (2021)</a>'s
        {D.descals_mapped_mha} Mha mapped / {D.descals_estimate_mha} Mha estimated and
        <a href="#ref-gaveau">Gaveau et al. (2022)</a>'s {D.gaveau_mapped_mha} Mha mapped /
        {D.gaveau_adjusted_mha} Mha adjusted. It is <b>{D.sdpt_vs_descals}× Descals</b>, not a
        fraction of it. Whatever else is true, the conservatism argument does not hold, and the note
        has been corrected.</p>
        <p class="caveat"><b>A claim on the page itself that its data contradicts.</b> The page states
        that Java and Nusa Tenggara "return no alerts at all". They return
        {num(DI.java_ha, 1)} ha and {num(DI.nusa_ha, 1)} ha respectively —
        {pct(DI.java_share, 2)} and {pct(DI.nusa_share, 2)} of the national total, from
        {DI.provinces_with_alerts} of {DI.provinces_total} provinces that produce any. Vanishingly
        small, which is the point being made, but not zero. Corrected in §13.</p>
      </section>

      <section id="s10">
        <h2><span class="num">10</span> Finding seven — what "two-sensor agreement" is measuring</h2>
        <p>The case's two-sensor check reports that {pct(g3.agreement_share, 1)} of high-confidence
        events of at least 5 ha carry a GLAD-L optical alert within ±{S.window_days} days, and passes
        against a {pct(g3.threshold, 0)} threshold. That subset is {num(g3.n_clusters)} events —
        {pct(S.gate_event_share, 1)} of the record and {pct(S.gate_ha_share, 1)} of its hectares.</p>
        <figure>
          <Fragment set:html={F8} />
          <figcaption><b>Figure 8.</b> Optical confirmation rate by event size. Teal bars are inside
          the published check's ≥5 ha subset; the dashed rules are the published figure and the
          all-events figure. Hover a bar.</figcaption>
        </figure>
        <p>Confirmation rises monotonically with size, from {pct((S.by_size as any[])[0].agree, 1)} for
        {(S.by_size as any[])[0].band} ha events to {pct((S.by_size as any[])[5].agree, 1)} for the
        largest. Across all {num(R.events)} events it is {pct(S.agree_all_events, 1)} by count and
        {pct(S.agree_all_ha, 1)} by hectare. Two things are tangled here and the check does not
        separate them: a bigger clearing genuinely is easier for a 30 m optical sensor to see, and a
        bigger footprint contains more GLAD pixels spanning more dates, so the interval-overlap rule
        the check uses becomes easier to satisfy. The published {pct(g3.agreement_share, 1)} is
        therefore partly a property of the ≥5 ha filter.</p>
        <p>The check is also one-directional. It asks how many radar alerts optical confirms; it
        cannot ask how many optical alerts radar missed, because GLAD is only sampled inside RADD
        footprints. A shortfall here is evidence about optical, never about radar.</p>
        <figure>
          <Fragment set:html={F9} />
          <figcaption><b>Figure 9.</b> Days between the radar detection and the optical one, for the
          {num(S.lead.n_in_window)} events where both fired inside the agreement window. Hover a
          bar.</figcaption>
        </figure>
        <p>Where both sensors do fire, the radar-first case is what the case is built on and it holds:
        radar is first for <b>{pct(S.lead.share_radar_first, 0)}</b> of matched events, with a median
        lead of <b>{S.lead.median} days</b> (interquartile range {S.lead.p25} to {S.lead.p75}). In the
        wet tropics that is the difference between acting this month and acting next quarter, and it
        is the strongest single justification for a radar-first monitor. The remaining
        {pct(S.lead.out_of_window_share, 0)} of matched pairs sit outside the ±{S.window_days} day
        window entirely — usually because the same ground was disturbed in an earlier episode that
        GLAD recorded — so they are co-location, not concurrence, and are excluded from the lead
        statistic.</p>
      </section>

      <section id="s11">
        <h2><span class="num">11</span> Finding eight — what does reconcile, and what the checks are worth</h2>
        <p>Adversarial review is only credible if it also reports what survives. The plumbing here
        does.</p>
        <figure>
          <Fragment set:html={F10} />
          <figcaption><b>Figure 10.</b> Hectares recomputed from the raw tiles on this machine against
          Global Forest Watch's own published Indonesian totals. The primary-forest rows are computed
          here for the first time — the Hansen loss year crossed with the 2001 primary mask at 30 m —
          so they are an independent test of the geometry, the masking and the pixel-area
          weighting.</figcaption>
        </figure>
        <p>The case's tree-cover-loss reconciliation lands within {tclDiff24}% of GFW's published
        2024 figure, and this review's independent crossing reproduces the case's
        own published national loss series to within rounding. More usefully, the primary-forest loss computed
        here for the first time — Hansen loss year crossed with the 2001 primary mask at 30 m — lands
        within {L.primary_reconciliation_pct["2023"]}% and {L.primary_reconciliation_pct["2024"]}% of
        GFW's published Indonesian primary-forest loss for 2023 and 2024. That is a genuinely
        independent check on the geometry, the pixel-area weighting and the masking, and it passes.</p>
        <p>The four published checks are a mixed set once the base rate is in hand. The alert
        reconciliation (worst deviation {g2.max_abs_pct_diff.toFixed(2)}% against a ±{g2.tolerance_pct}%
        tolerance) is a real test of the raster plumbing and it earned its keep — the repository
        records that it is what caught a clustering bug that had back-dated most of the archive into
        2020. The tree-cover-loss reconciliation is reported <b>failed</b>, on a single province-year
        of {g1.worst.ours_ha.toFixed(2)} ha against {g1.worst.gfw_ha.toFixed(2)} ha in Jakarta;
        {pct(g1.within_tolerance_share, 1)} of {num(g1.n_comparisons)} comparisons pass and every one
        of the {num(g1.n_material)} above {num(g1.materiality_floor_ha)} ha does. Publishing that as a
        failure rather than re-cutting the tolerance is the right call and we would not change it.</p>
        <p class="caveat">The two remaining checks are weaker than they look. The two-sensor check
        measures a filtered subset whose pass rate is partly the filter (§10). The linkage check
        cannot fail, for the reason in §5. Neither is wrong; both need their denominator printed
        beside them, which is what §13 does.</p>
      </section>

      <section id="s12">
        <h2><span class="num">12</span> What follows for decisions</h2>
        <p>Evidence is only worth gathering if it changes an action. Read strictly, this instrument
        supports four uses and forbids a fifth.</p>
        <ol class="uses">
          <li><b>Weekly triage on the frontier.</b> Where the lift is {nkal.lift_50}× or
          {wpap.lift_50}× — North Kalimantan, West Papua, Papua — a catchment flag genuinely narrows
          the search. For a provincial forestry office in Kalimantan or an NGO monitoring team, the
          ranked weekly list is the product, and the {S.lead.median}-day radar lead over optical is
          what makes it a weekly list rather than a quarterly one.</li>
          <li><b>Peat exposure, which is the highest-consequence flag on the page.</b>
          {pct(R.peat_share, 1)} of alerted hectares are on peat and {pct(R.peat_in_catchment, 1)} of
          those are inside a catchment — a higher rate than alerts as a whole. Indonesia's peat
          moratorium and the EUDR both make peat clearing categorically different from other
          clearing, and this is the one flag where a spatial coincidence maps onto a legal boundary
          rather than a commercial hypothesis.</li>
          <li><b>Due-diligence triage, explicitly labelled as triage.</b> Regulation (EU) 2023/1115
          requires plot-level geolocation — a polygon for any plot over 4 ha — and a cut-off of
          31 December 2020, which is almost exactly where this alert record begins. A
          {R.mill_radius_km} km circle is the pre-EUDR proxy the regulation was written to replace.
          It cannot discharge due diligence; it can tell an operator which of its declared plots to
          check first, and which weeks to check them in.</li>
          <li><b>The definitional argument, which this case wins.</b> Chapter 01 already refuses to
          publish one headline hectare figure. §4 now puts a number on why:
          {pct(L.grand_plantation_share, 1)} of the loss is inside land already mapped as plantation.
          For KLHK, for a ministry press office, and for anyone arguing about whether Indonesian
          deforestation is 175,400 ha or 1.1 Mha, that decomposition is more useful than either
          number alone.</li>
          <li class="no"><b>Not: attributing clearing to a named mill or group.</b> With
          {ID.mills.mean_over_alert_ha_in_catchment.toFixed(2)} mills per hectare, a
          {H.lift}× lift and a pressure column that sums to {ID.double_count}× the hectares that
          exist, the ranking cannot support a statement about any individual mill's conduct. It is a
          screening order. Naming groups next to it invites exactly the reading the page's own caveat
          disclaims.</li>
        </ol>
        <p class="pull">The honest reframing is small and it makes the case stronger: this is not
        "deforestation as a supply-chain question", it is <i>a weekly triage instrument whose value is
        highest exactly where the supply chain is least mapped</i>. That is the gap between having
        data and acting on it — which is the axis the work is meant to sit on.</p>
      </section>

      <section id="s13">
        <h2><span class="num">13</span> Corrections applied to the case page</h2>
        <p>Everything in this section has been changed on <a href={`${BASE}/`}>the instrument</a>
        itself, not merely recommended.</p>
        <ol class="next">
          <li><b>The headline now carries its denominator.</b> The linkage tile reads
          {pct(H.alert, 1)} against a base rate of {pct(H.domain_base, 1)} and a lift of {H.lift}×,
          and the {C.best_radius.km} km class is published beside the {R.mill_radius_km} km one.</li>
          <li><b>The linkage check is relabelled.</b> It was presented as a literature-floor test
          against Gaveau et al.'s {pct(LIT.gaveau.palmShareOfLoss, 0)} direct-conversion figure. A
          proximity share cannot be tested against a conversion share, and in Riau the check could not
          fail at any threshold below {pct(riau.domain_base_50, 0)}. It is now stated as a lift test
          with Riau's base rate printed beside it.</li>
          <li><b>"Java and Nusa Tenggara return no alerts at all" is corrected</b> to the true figures
          — {num(DI.java_ha, 1)} ha and {num(DI.nusa_ha, 1)} ha, together
          {pct((DI.java_share as number) + (DI.nusa_share as number), 2)} of the national total.</li>
          <li><b>The national event-floor share is published.</b> The page previously gave only the
          three-province figure ({pct(F.gate_keeps_focus_provinces, 0)}); the national figure is
          {pct(F.keeps, 1)}, and the unfiltered catchment share {pct(F.unfiltered_within_50, 1)} is
          now stated next to the filtered one.</li>
          <li><b>The mill ranking is relabelled a screening order</b> and carries the number of mills
          sharing each hectare, so the column is not read as additive hectares.</li>
          <li><b>The SDPT extent note is corrected</b> in the repository: SDPT maps
          {D.sdpt_vs_descals}× Descals' Indonesian oil-palm extent, so the palm-linked share is not
          conservative for the reason previously given.</li>
        </ol>
        <p>Two things we deliberately did <i>not</i> change. The failed tree-cover-loss reconciliation
        stays failed and stays published. And the {R.mill_radius_km} km radius stays, because it is
        the convention the industry and Trase use; it now simply travels with its base rate.</p>
      </section>

